Optimal Solution - Greedy solution that validates whether the target triplet can be formed by combining valid triplets without exceeding the target values.
                   I ignore invalid triplets and greedily track which target coordinates can be matched; if all three are covered, the merge is possible.

class Solution {
    public boolean mergeTriplets(int[][] triplets, int[] target) {
        // Set to track which indices (0, 1, 2) of target can be matched
        Set<Integer> good = new HashSet<>();
        // Iterate through each triplet
        for (int[] t : triplets) {
            // Skip this triplet if it exceeds target in any dimension
            if (t[0] > target[0] || t[1] > target[1] || t[2] > target[2]) {
                continue;
            }
            // Check each position of the triplet
            for (int i = 0; i < t.length; i++) {
                // If this triplet matches target at index i,
                // mark this index as achievable
                if (t[i] == target[i]) {
                    good.add(i);
                }
            }
        }
        // If all 3 indices are matched, target can be formed
        return good.size() == 3;
    }
}


Time Complexity :- O(N).
Space Complexity :- O(1).
